Backpressure occurs when the producer (readable) is faster than the consumer (writable). Data accumulates in memory. Without handling, you get high memory and eventually OOM.

       How streams handle it
- writable.write(chunk) returns false when the internal buffer exceeds highWaterMark. That means "stop writing."
- When the writable drains its buffer, it emits 'drain'. Then you can resume reading/writing.

       Manual handling
if (!writable.write(chunk)) {
  readable.pause();
  writable.once('drain', () => readable.resume());
}

       Automatic readable.pipe(writable) handles this. It pauses the readable when the writable returns false and resumes on 'drain'.

       Pitfall Custom writable streams must respect the return value of write and emit 'drain' when ready. Otherwise backpressure breaks.

